[size=5][b]Tier 7 Expansion[/b][/size]

[size=4][b]Description[/b][/size]

You finish the last magazine, the crafting skill hits its cap, and from that day on the game has nothing left to hand you. Every gun you loot is a repeat, every craft comes out the same, and quality 6 is where your character stops improving for the rest of the playthrough.

Tier 7 Expansion adds one more step. Every weapon, tool, armour piece, clothing piece and item mod in the game gains a seventh quality: one extra mod slot, every tier-scaled number continuing at the same step vanilla uses from 1 to 6, and a gold band above legendary purple. The crafting skills go past 100 to make room for it, so the magazines you find after capping out have somewhere to go.

No new items, no new weapon types, no reworked loot tables. It extends what is already in the game by exactly one tier.

[size=4][b]Main features[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]172 items reach quality 7.[/b] 43 weapons - junk sledge, turret and drone included - 16 tools, 66 armour pieces, 12 clothing pieces and 35 item mods.
[*][b]One more mod slot.[/b] Where tier 6 gives four, tier 7 gives five.
[*][b]Stats continue, they do not jump.[/b] Every ladder gains one more rung of the size vanilla was already using, so tier 7 is a real step and not a new balance.
[*][b]Crafted or looted.[/b] Craft it at the new skill cap for six Legendary Parts, or find it in loot five times less often than tier 6. Traders never stock it.
[*][b]Crafting skills go to 60, 90 and 120[/b] instead of 50, 75 and 100 - a fifth of the ladder added on top. Everything you have already unlocked stays exactly where it was.
[*][b]Armour set bonuses continue too.[/b] A full tier 7 set keeps its bonus and steps it up, rather than losing it for being past the last number vanilla wrote down.
[*][b]Junk stays junk.[/b] Pipe guns, primitive weapons, stone tools and primitive armour get tier 7 for free and gain no extra slot.
[*][b]Works on an existing save.[/b] No new items means nothing to migrate.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Where tier 7 comes from[/b][/size]

Crafting needs two things: the crafting skill for that family sitting at its new cap, and six Legendary Parts, the same component tier 6 asks one of. There is no new crafting component to farm.

It also drops. One would-be quality 6 drop in six comes out as quality 7 instead, which makes tier 7 five times rarer than tier 6 without changing how often weapons drop at all. A looted one rolls its stats the way any looted item does, starred band included; a crafted one is plain, exactly like every other crafted quality in the game.

Item mods are loot only. Vanilla gives mod recipes no crafting tier at all, so a crafted mod is quality 1 whatever your skill - that is not something this mod changes.

[size=4][b]The magazines[/b][/size]

Nothing about them changes except that the last stretch takes more of them: each still gives one level, and the level cap is what moved. Reaching tier 7 in a family is the same grind as reaching tier 6 was, extended by a fifth.

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]7 Days to Die [b]V 3.2.0[/b]. Built and tested against b9.
[*]The game must run [b]without EasyAntiCheat[/b]. The mod ships a DLL and EAC blocks those - launch 7DaysToDie.exe directly, or pick the non-EAC option in the Steam launcher.
[*][b]Crafting Max Quality[/b] in Sandbox Options left on [b]Default[/b]. Setting it to an explicit 6 caps crafting at 6, and tier 7 is then only reachable from loot.
[*]No other mods needed. Harmony comes from the game's own 0_TFP_Harmony.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installation instructions[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Download the archive.
[*]Extract it into your Mods folder so that you end up with [b]Mods\Tier7Expansion\ModInfo.xml[/b]. If your game has no Mods folder yet, create one next to 7DaysToDie.exe.
[*]Start the game without EasyAntiCheat.
[/list]

Vortex and the 7D2D Mod Launcher handle the archive as a normal modlet.

To uninstall, delete the folder. The mod writes nothing of its own into your save - but any quality 7 item you are holding keeps that quality with no tier 7 defined for it any more, which means it loses the bonuses and the mod slot the tier gave it until the mod is back. Scrap or hand off your tier 7 gear before removing it.

[size=4][b]Multiplayer[/b][/size]

This one is not client side. It changes item definitions, and both ends read those from their own files, so the [b]server and every client need the same version installed[/b].

[b]Only tested in single player so far.[/b] Feedback from a dedicated server is welcome.

[size=4][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]

Every config change is an XPath patch pinned to the exact vanilla value it expects. If another mod already rewrote a weapon's mod slots or its damage ladder, the patch finds nothing and that weapon keeps their numbers instead of this mod's - it loses the argument rather than winning it. The quality cap is raised from code with a maximum rather than a fixed value, so load order cannot make this mod stomp one that wants tier 8.

Overhauls that rebalance weapons and armour wholesale, such as Darkness Falls or Undead Legacy, replace the ladders this mod extends; expect most of it to simply not apply there.

Two armour set bonuses and one weapon mod repeat their tier 6 numbers at tier 7 rather than stepping up. Vanilla writes those three as separate per-tier blocks whose numbers are not a ladder that can be read, and repeating the top value is better than inventing one.

[size=4][b]The creative menu[/b][/size]

Type [b]#7[/b] in the creative menu search box to get tier 7 of everything. [b]giveself <item> 7[/b] works too, and skips the loot rolls, which makes it the clean way to compare two tiers side by side.

